From 9003c56c20f46b8f3e816557758a378099f4c30a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:59:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] doc/examples/encode_audio: keep the phase of the tone in a
 double

The phase of the generated 440 Hz tone is accumulated in a float for
230400 samples. Near the end the phase is above 14000 radians, where a
float can no longer represent the per-sample increment of 0.0627
accurately, so the tone drifts. Measured on the decoded output by
counting zero crossings over one second:

    at 0.2 s: 440.30 Hz
    at 2.0 s: 438.67 Hz
    at 4.0 s: 439.03 Hz

With a double the tone stays at 440.00 Hz.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FZ4G6798n6Ua4qu6xwWu3S
---
 doc/examples/encode_audio.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/doc/examples/encode_audio.c b/doc/examples/encode_audio.c
index d4d756c..0e68b54 100644
--- a/doc/examples/encode_audio.c
+++ b/doc/examples/encode_audio.c
@@ -151,7 +151,7 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
     int i, j, k, ret;
     FILE *f;
     uint16_t *samples;
-    float t, tincr;
+    double t, tincr;
 
     if (argc <= 1) {
         fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <output file>\n", argv[0]);
